Dated 2.1.84. This page continues a combinatorial set-up from the previous page. There Grothendieck had a cell complex K, apparently a cell decomposition of the sphere or projective plane coming from a system of pseudolines. Its ingredients were faces F, edges ('arêtes') α and vertices S(K). It carried a distinguished subset L, which looks like the trace of another system of pseudolines or a chosen union of lines. He had also introduced a set C_K of 'critical points' on the 1-skeleton, each with a weight ν(s) ≥ 1, signs ε_F, and an antipodal map on each face boundary ∂F.

Here he refines the structure by placing two sets of marked points, S'_K and S''_K, on K. The coloured drawings suggest S' points are the black/red dots and S'' points mark one point per side.

(b, boxed) For every face F, the points of S' on ∂F cut ∂F into a topological polygon of order 2α_x. Each of its sides carries exactly one point of S'', which is the side's midpoint under a condition that is hard to read. This is a barycentric-type, centrally symmetric marking of each face boundary. The 2α_x-gon and the antipodal structure fit faces of a double cover of RP^2 or of an antipodally symmetric picture.

(a) S'_K ∩ L = (K ∩ L) minus the critical points of K of odd order (probably: of odd weight ν).

(b) For every ordinary edge α of K, α ∩ S'_K ≠ ∅ in one case; in the opposite case S'_K picks out the unique vertex of K on α.

He then notes that a) and b) imply S'_K ∩ S(K) = S(K) ∩ L: a vertex of K is in S'_K iff it lies in L.

(c) This defines a combinatorial index for a critical point s on an edge α. Let t', t'' be the points of α ∩ L nearest to s on either side. Then the number of S'_K points in [t', t''] is set equal to i(s), the index of s; this should tie back to the weights ν(s) of the previous page.

The drawings illustrate the intended picture:
- red hexagon, pentagon and heptagon faces with marked boundary points;
- a green 'flow' from an orange interior point to boundary points;
- one-dimensional local models of critical points (a red cross on a green dotted segment, a corner);
- a green zig-zag path crossing a bundle of red lines, i.e. a pseudoline weaving through an arrangement, with orange critical points at the turns;
- a disk model of the projective plane with chords and thickened boundary arcs.

The overall aim seems to be a discrete theory of 'critical points/inflections with index' for a pseudoline or curve relative to an arrangement. That theory would include:
- lower bounds (previous page: at least 3, and at least 4 in some case, critical points on each ∂F);
- antipodal symmetry relations for the signs;
- (next page) how critical points and signs change when passing from a face F to σ_-(F).

This is reminiscent of a combinatorial Möbius/Sturm-type theorem (inflection counts on projective curves). The page itself is definitional: it fixes the marking conventions and the index, and proves only the trivial consequence S'_K ∩ S(K) = S(K) ∩ L.
